   00964| /// Whether the payload starting at `from` is a block that outlives its first line.
   00965| ///
   00966| /// A marker alone on its line already introduces the whole block below it, and
   00967| /// that is the shape this route was built for. The same request written with the
   00968| /// payload starting on the marker's own line -- "with exactly this content: #
   00969| /// Title\n\nbody..." -- means exactly the same thing, but the sentence bound cut
   00970| /// it at the first line and delivered a 58-byte file for a 1478-byte document.
   00971| ///
   00972| /// That is not a bound the author of the request can see. It made every
   00973| /// multi-line literal write silently lossy: `alpha\nbeta\ngamma` was written as
   00974| /// `alpha`, and the self-authoring loop produced a one-line stub of the document
   00975| /// it was handed, which is how it looked broken while reporting success.
   00976| ///
   00977| /// A payload whose first line ends but whose block continues is therefore read
   00978| /// to `limit`, exactly as the marker-only spelling always was. The sentence bound
   00979| /// still governs a payload that stays on one line, so "write the following: hello
   00980| /// to `x.txt`" is unaffected -- there is no continuation to find.
   00981| pub(super) fn payload_continues_past_its_first_line(
   00982|     request: &str,
   00983|     from: usize,
   00984|     sentence_end: usize,
   00985| ) -> bool {
   00986|     let Some(tail) = request.get(from..) else {
   00987|         return false;
   00988|     };
   00989|     let Some(break_at) = tail.find('\n') else {
   00990|         return false;
   00991|     };
   00992|     // Only a payload the sentence bound would actually truncate qualifies: the
   00993|     // newline has to fall inside the sentence being cut, and real content has to
   00994|     // follow it. Otherwise the sentence bound is already returning the whole
   00995|     // payload and there is nothing to widen.
   00996|     from + break_at < sentence_end && tail[break_at..].chars().any(char::is_alphanumeric)
   00997| }
